ered, (2,3) covered, (3,1) not covered, (3,2) not covered. Actually we haven't covered (3,1) and (3,2). So we need to cover them as well. But we have used all 4 lines. So this fails.\n\nWe could try different pairings to include (3,1) and (3,2). Let's try to pair (3,1) with (1,2) maybe? Slope (2-1)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, sunny. But (1,2) already used in L2. We could reassign.\n\nLet's try to find a set of 4 lines covering all 10 points. Since we have 10 points, each line must cover on average 2.5 points. So at least some lines must cover 3 points. Let's see if any line can cover 3 points that are not vertical/horizontal/slope -1. For example, line through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) would be slope 1, but (3,3) is not a point in our set (since (3,3) would have a+b=6 > n+1=5). So not present. Maybe line through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) but (3,0) not in set (b must be positive). So maybe no line can cover 3 points.\n\nBut maybe a line can cover 3 points like (1,1),(2,3),(3,5) but (3,5) not in set. So likely no line can cover 3 points. Let's check systematically: For any line not vertical/horizontal/slope -1, can it pass through three points of the set? The set is a triangular lattice. The only lines that pass through three points of a triangular lattice are those with slope 0, infinite, -1, 1, 2, 1/2, etc. But we need to check if any such line passes through three points within the triangle.\n\nConsider slope 1 lines: they have points with constant x-y. In our set, each difference appears at most twice (except maybe difference 0 appears twice). So slope 1 lines can have at most 2 points.\n\nConsider slope 2 lines: points with constant (y-2x) maybe. Let's compute y-2x for each point:\n\n- (1,1): -1\n- (1,2): 0\n- (1,3): 1\n- (1,4): 2\n- (2,1): -3\n- (2,2): -2\n- (2,3): -1\n- (3,1): -5\n- (3,2): -4\n- (4,1): -7\n\nWe see each value appears at most twice? Actually -1 appears for (1,1) and (2,3). So slope 2 line (y = 2x -1) passes through (1,1) and (2,3). That's two points. No third point 